# kgsm-rag-indexer — configuration (systemd EnvironmentFile format: KEY=VALUE, # comments).
#
# Lives at /etc/kgsm-assistant/rag-indexer.env. The unit reads it with EnvironmentFile=-, so an
# absent file is not an error and the unit's own Environment= defaults apply.
#
# Only these two keys exist. Everything else the indexer runs on — the corpus, the index file, the
# model, the debounce — is a CLI flag in the unit's ExecStart, because those are properties of what
# is being indexed rather than of the host. Change one by editing the unit.
#
# deploy/llama-server/use-backend.sh REWRITES this whole file when the inference backend moves. It
# is the intended way to set both keys: they have to agree with what the assistant is pointed at,
# and setting one alone builds the index against a server the assistant does not use.

# ── Which embedding server to build the index against ────────────────────────
# The indexer holds no provider switch of its own — it is told. Ollama and llama-server expose
# different embedding routes, so guessing would trade a failed build for a wrong index.
#
#   LlamaCpp   kgsm-llama-embed.service
#   Ollama     ollama.service
#                                                                    [LlamaCpp]
#RAG_EMBED_PROVIDER=LlamaCpp

# Where that server listens. The llama.cpp embedder's port is LLAMA_EMBED_PORT in
# llama-server.env; Ollama serves embeddings on its own endpoint, http://127.0.0.1:11434.
#                                                     [http://127.0.0.1:8082]
#RAG_EMBED_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8082

# Changing the embedding backend does NOT rebuild the index. The indexer is incremental by
# content hash and the index header records the model NAME, which does not change when the server
# behind it does — so a switch reports "0 embedded, N reused" and keeps vectors from the previous
# embedder, in a different vector space, with nothing reporting an error. Delete the .krag under
# /var/lib/kgsm-assistant and let the indexer rebuild it.
